tests/syscalls — the pinned per-op syscall surface. Tier 10a, tools/bscalls.sh.

One file per case per platform, holding the calls that case made BEYOND the
broker's own plumbing, counted, from the fork onwards. Most of them are empty,
and an empty file is the strongest statement in this directory: that fixture
asked the kernel for nothing at all.

The two directories are not expected to agree, and that is the point of having
two. Which calls are visible to a tracer is a platform property rather than a
program property, and both halves of that turned out to be more interesting
than expected:

  time.live   Linux measures NOTHING. glibc serves clock_gettime from the
              vDSO, so the syscall is never made and no tracer can see one.
              FreeBSD measures TWO, one per clock read. The guess that FreeBSD
              would also serve this from its timekeeping page was wrong, and
              was corrected by the gate rather than by reasoning.

  rand.live   Linux measures one getrandom. FreeBSD measures three calls, and
              the two extra ones are the interesting part: arc4random_buf
              allocates its per-thread generator state on FIRST USE -- an mmap,
              then a minherit to mark the page INHERIT_ZERO so a fork cannot
              inherit a generator's state. That is a one-time lazy
              initialisation that happens to land inside the measured window,
              because the window opens at the fork and the first random_bytes
              comes after it.

              IT IS PINNED AS OBSERVED RATHER THAN ENGINEERED AWAY, for now.
              Drawing a few bytes at startup would move all three calls out of
              the window and make every random_bytes look alike, which is the
              right thing to do -- but it is an M8 prerequisite rather than an
              M3 tidy-up, because the reason it matters is that a lockdown
              filter installed before the loop would otherwise have to permit
              mmap and minherit forever for the sake of one lazy init. See
              HANDOFF, "What is next".

              Note what this costs on the primary platform: after that first
              call FreeBSD's generator is pure userspace, so rand.live and
              rand.seeded would measure the same once warmed. The pair only
              demonstrates "a seed consults no kernel" on Linux. Tier 7 proves
              the determinism itself and does not depend on either.

PROVENANCE:

  linux/    measured with `sh tools/bscalls.sh --record` on ubuntu-26.04.
  freebsd/  measured on freebsd-15.1 through the gate. The first version of
            these files was written from what the platform documents and two
            of the five were wrong, which is recorded in HANDOFF rather than
            quietly corrected.

To re-pin a platform after a deliberate change: sh tools/bscalls.sh --record,
and say in the commit message what moved and why. When a pin cannot be
measured from the host doing the work, say so IN THE FILE, the way the first
version of this one did -- the gate then corrects a labelled guess instead of
appearing to contradict a measurement.
